NAS) by sharing weights among different child models is a popular strategy. However, due to interference from other models, there is often a poor correlation between predicted accuracy and actual performance of the models. This study investigates the cause of interference by defining equivalent relationships between models and proposes a method to randomize the selection of top models to overcome it. Furthermore, we present a novel cost-effective technique to concurrently train and evaluate multiple models, allowing for independent assessment of each model's performance. This independent assessment leads to significantly better hit rates compared to the original method. Utilizing this approach, we developed the superCIFS architecture search space, yielding a novel mobile net architecture, mnet-superCIFS-1.25, which outperforms mnet-1.25 by 0.46% in top-1 accuracy, despite having comparable parameters and an 83.0% reduction in computation. This new architecture is suitable for deployment on mobile and edge devices. The source code is publicly available at https://github.com/visual-embeddings/mnet-superCIFS. 

